Tension Headache
Young Adult & Adolecence
Bilateral band-like pain, Non-throbbing character, Pericranial muscle tenderness, Absence of nausea/vomiting

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-045
Reason for Visit: Acute escalation of severe, persistent head pain leading to profound academic distress and anxiety regarding a potential brain tumor.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient experiencing a severe, unyielding daily headache that has recently escalated, causing acute psychological distress and a fear of malignant intracranial pathology. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was necessary to perform a comprehensive neurological examination to definitively rule out signs of raised intracranial pressure or a space-occupying lesion, and to provide immediate clinical reassurance and mechanical pain relief prior to morning regular GP hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My head feels like it is being crushed in a vice every single afternoon, and my neck is so stiff I'm terrified I have a brain tumor."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a 4-week history of an insidious onset of head pain, typically peaking in the late afternoon. The pain is described as a bilateral tightening, pressing, or band-like sensation wrapped around the head. While historically mild to moderate, the intensity has spiked over the last 3 days due to extreme stress regarding upcoming university examinations. The pain is strictly non-throbbing. The patient explicitly notes an absence of nausea, vomiting, severe photophobia, or phonophobia. The pain is not aggravated by routine physical exertion (e.g., climbing stairs). The acute panic tonight was triggered by the relentless nature of the pain and the profound stiffness and pericranial muscle tenderness in the neck and shoulders.
Functional Status: Independent, but highly distressed, fatigued, and struggling to maintain academic focus.
Social/Safety Concerns: High acute health anxiety; severe academic stress acting as a continuous environmental trigger.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 122/78 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Patient appears exhausted, anxious, and is actively massaging their temples and the back of their neck.
Physical Exam: A comprehensive neurological examination is completely unremarkable. Cranial nerves II-XII are intact. Fundoscopy reveals sharp optic discs with zero evidence of papilloedema, definitively ruling out raised intracranial pressure. Motor, sensory, and cerebellar testing are normal. There is no nuchal rigidity (Kernig's/Brudzinski's are negative). Musculoskeletal examination reveals severe, exquisite pericranial muscle tenderness on palpation of the scalp, bilateral temporalis muscles, posterior cervical paraspinals, and upper trapezius. The clinical presentation perfectly aligns with the diagnostic tetrad of bilateral band-like pain, non-throbbing character, pericranial muscle tenderness, and the strict absence of nausea/vomiting.
Environmental Observations: Patient's study area is highly unergonomic, featuring a laptop placed low on a desk, forcing chronic severe cervical flexion.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient has been taking Paracetamol 1000mg intermittently with minimal sustained relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Severe Episodic Tension-Type Headache - Status: Exacerbated by acute stress and poor ergonomics
Differentials:

Space-Occupying Lesion (Brain Tumor) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the completely normal neurological examination, normal fundoscopy (no papilloedema), and the pain classically peaking in the afternoon (muscle fatigue) rather than presenting as severe early morning headaches with vomiting.

Migraine - Ruled out/unlikely due to the strictly bilateral, non-throbbing nature of the pain, and the explicit absence of nausea, vomiting, and severe visual aura or photophobia.

Acute Meningitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the complete absence of fever, the lack of true nuchal rigidity (inability to touch chin to chest without pain), and the chronic 4-week history of symptoms.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (High psychological risk regarding health anxiety)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presented with severe, escalating daily head pain driven by chronic muscular tension and acute academic stress, culminating in extreme health anxiety regarding a brain tumor. The immediate after-hours home visit was clinically justified by the necessity to physically exclude signs of raised intracranial pressure and focal neurological deficits. The comprehensive clinical examination safely and confidently excluded acute intracranial pathology, confirming that the severe pain is secondary to mechanical pericranial muscle spasm. Delaying assessment would have prolonged the patient's severe anxiety and pain. The visit provided a definitive, benign mechanical explanation for the headaches, instantly de-escalating the crisis and allowing for targeted physical interventions.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Provided direct, factual clinical reassurance that the neurological exam is completely normal, explicitly confirming the absence of signs of a brain tumor. Administered Ibuprofen 400mg PO stat to provide targeted anti-inflammatory relief to the spastic musculature. Advised the patient that future neuroimaging is not clinically indicated based on the benign exam.
Positive Behavioral Change: To physically break the severe muscle spasm causing the headache, apply a heat pack directly to the back of the neck and shoulders for 20 minutes every evening, actively utilizing thermal vasodilation to relax the tight muscle fibers. To prevent the daily recurrence of the mechanical strain, immediately elevate your laptop screen to eye level using books, explicitly eliminating the constant downward neck angle that is actively tearing your cervical muscles during study.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks to assess the response to ergonomic changes and discuss stress management strategies; referral to a physiotherapist strongly recommended for deep tissue release.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to the Emergency Department is required if the headache suddenly becomes "explosive" (the worst pain of your life within seconds), if you develop severe vomiting, or if you experience sudden weakness, numbness, or difficulty speaking.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The clinical examination is entirely normal, confirming these are severe tension headaches; scans are not currently required.
Clinical Course: The headaches will persist until the physical stress on your neck muscles is removed via proper posture and heat therapy, but the immediate pain will lessen with anti-inflammatory medication.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency medical attention if your headache suddenly changes to a severe, explosive pain, if you begin vomiting repeatedly, or if you develop a high fever and cannot bend your neck forward.

